# Lightweight by default

This project must stay **simple, fast, and cheap to run and to change**. Prefer understanding the existing structure before adding anything.

## Always

- **Just do it.** Apply edits, commits, and deploys without waiting for the user to review files. Don’t ask them to approve diffs unless something is destructive or unclear.
- **Read first.** Skim the relevant files and routes so changes fit what already exists.
- **Smallest change that works.** No new frameworks, layers, helpers, or abstractions unless asked.
- **Low load.** Avoid heavy deps, big client bundles, extra API routes, polling, or build complexity.
- **Low network/token use.** Prefer existing local assets; avoid adding new external fetches or third-party scripts.
- **Low tokens / noise.** Don’t rewrite whole files, dump long plans, or add docs the user didn’t ask for.
- **Clear architecture.** Put code where this repo already puts it (`app/` for pages, `public/` for static assets). Don’t invent parallel systems.

## Never (unless explicitly asked)

- Over-engineer (design systems, state libraries, config sprawl, “future-proof” wrappers)
- Duplicate patterns that already exist in the lesson or homepage
- Add features, refactors, or comments unrelated to the request
- Burn tokens re-explaining or re-scanning the whole repo when a narrow edit will do

## Decision test

If two approaches work, pick the one with **fewer files, fewer deps, and less runtime work**.
